{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujin","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yujin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesup","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taesup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Free Continual Learning with Null Space Adaptation for Zero-Shot Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T03:41:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:33:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot generalization, enabling deployment in a wide range of real-world tasks without additional task-specific training. However, in real deployment scenarios with evolving environments or emerging classes, these models inevitably face distributional shifts and novel tasks. In such contexts, static zero-shot capabilities are insufficient, and there is a growing need for continual learning methods that allow models to adapt over time while avoiding catastrophic forgetting. We introduce NuSA-CL (Null Space Adaptation for Continual Learning), a lightweight memory-free continual learning framework designed to address this challenge. NuSA-CL employs low-rank adaptation and constrains task-specific weight updates to lie within an approximate null space of the model's current parameters. This strategy minimizes interference with previously acquired knowledge, effectively preserving the zero-shot capabilities of the original model. Unlike methods relying on replay buffers or costly distillation, NuSA-CL imposes minimal computational and memory overhead, making it practical for deployment in resource-constrained, real-world continual learning environments. Experiments show that our framework not only effectively preserves zero-shot transfer capabilities but also achieves highly competitive performance on continual learning benchmarks. These results position NuSA-CL as a practical and scalable solution for continually evolving zero-shot VLMs in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/ZoYujin/NuSA-CL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haobin","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Haobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MediRec: Enhancing Chinese Medication Recommendation with Explainable Clinical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:47:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T09:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:25:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T07:53:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:31:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong potential for clinical decision support through their advanced language understanding and reasoning capabilities. However, their application to Chinese clinical medication recommendation remains largely unexplored. Existing approaches are primarily developed on English electronic health record datasets and focus on coarse-grained medication code prediction, offering limited support for interpretable clinical decision-making. In this work, we propose MediRec, an explainable LLM-based framework for Chinese medication recommendation from electronic health records. MediRec combines clinically grounded reasoning-chain distillation with reinforcement learning to improve both recommendation accuracy and interpretability. Comprehensive experiments on a Chinese medication recommendation benchmark show that MediRec achieves strong performance, with an F1 score of 0.5813 and a Jaccard score of 0.4626. Further analyses indicate that MediRec generates clinically plausible recommendations with transparent reasoning, demonstrating its effectiveness for explainable medication decision support in Chinese healthcare settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NLPCC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:22Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:14:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghui","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Penghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Sinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongzhe","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changqian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Changqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Gai","name":"Gai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiantian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xueqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Principled RL for Flow Matching Emerges from the Chunk-level Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:50:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T09:16:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:44:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T17:34:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:09:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Progress in post-training flow matching for text-to-image (T2I) generation with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) has demonstrated strong potential. However, it is hindered by a critical limitation: inaccurate advantage attribution. In this work, we argue that aggregating consecutive steps into a coherent 'chunk' and shifting the policy optimization paradigm from GRPO's step level to the chunk level can effectively mitigate the negative impact of this issue. Building on this insight, we propose Group Chunking Policy Optimization (GCPO), the first chunk-level reinforcement learning approach for post-training flow matching. Extensive experiments demonstrate that GCPO achieves superior performance on both standard T2I benchmarks and preference alignment, with up to 43% relative gains over GRPO, highlighting the promise of chunk-level policy optimization. The code is available on https://github.com/xingzhejun/GCPO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:02Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:20:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoqi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhanced Evolutionary Multi-Objective Deep Reinforcement Learning for Reliable and Efficient Wireless Rechargeable Sensor Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:30:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T13:53:33Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The article content needs to be significantly revised"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:55:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite rapid advancements in sensor networks, conventional battery-powered sensor networks suffer from limited operational lifespans and frequent maintenance requirements that severely constrain their deployment in remote and inaccessible environments. As such, wireless rechargeable sensor networks (WRSNs) with mobile charging capabilities offer a promising solution to extend network lifetime. However, WRSNs face critical challenges from the inherent trade-off between maximizing the node survival rates and maximizing charging energy efficiency under dynamic operational conditions. In this paper, we investigate a typical scenario where mobile chargers move and charge the sensor, thereby maintaining the network connectivity while minimizing the energy waste. Specifically, we formulate a multi-objective optimization problem that simultaneously maximizes the network node survival rate and mobile charger energy usage efficiency across multiple time slots, which presents NP-hard computational complexity with long-term temporal dependencies that make traditional optimization approaches ineffective. To address these challenges, we propose an enhanced evolutionary multi-objective deep reinforcement learning algorithm, which integrates a long short-term memory (LSTM)-based policy network for temporal pattern recognition, a multilayer perceptron-based prospective increment model for future state prediction, and a time-varying Pareto policy evaluation method for dynamic preference adaptation. Extensive simulation results demonstrate that the proposed algorithm significantly outperforms existing approaches in balancing node survival rate and energy efficiency while generating diverse Pareto-optimal solutions. Moreover, the LSTM-enhanced policy network converges 25% faster than conventional networks, with the time-varying evaluation method effectively adapting to dynamic conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The article content needs to be significantly revised"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:22Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:20:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longtian","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Longtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoisyGRPO: Incentivizing Multimodal CoT Reasoning via Noise Injection and Bayesian Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:23:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T07:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:32:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T14:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:26:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has shown promise in enhancing the general Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning capabilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs). However, when applied to improve general CoT reasoning, existing RL frameworks often struggle to generalize beyond the training distribution. To address this, we propose NoisyGRPO, a systematic multimodal RL framework that introduces controllable noise into visual inputs for enhanced exploration and explicitly models the advantage estimation process via a Bayesian framework. Specifically, NoisyGRPO improves RL training by: (1) Noise-Injected Exploration Policy: Perturbing visual inputs with Gaussian noise to encourage exploration across a wider range of visual scenarios; and (2) Bayesian Advantage Estimation: Formulating advantage estimation as a principled Bayesian inference problem, where the injected noise level serves as a prior and the observed trajectory reward as the likelihood. This Bayesian modeling fuses both sources of information to compute a robust posterior estimate of trajectory advantage, effectively guiding MLLMs to prefer visually grounded trajectories over noisy ones. Experiments on standard CoT quality, general capability, and hallucination benchmarks demonstrate that NoisyGRPO substantially improves generalization and robustness, especially in RL settings with small-scale MLLMs such as Qwen2.5-VL 3B. The project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Neurips 2025, Project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:15Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Schweter","name":"Schweter, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SindBERT, the Sailor: Charting the Seas of Turkish NLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T11:38:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T02:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.sigturk-1.1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models have revolutionized NLP, yet many morphologically rich languages remain underrepresented in large-scale pre-training efforts. With SindBERT, we set out to chart the seas of Turkish NLP, providing the first large-scale RoBERTa-based encoder for Turkish. Trained from scratch on 312~GB of Turkish text (mC4, OSCAR23, Wikipedia), SindBERT is released in both base and large configurations, representing the first large-scale encoder-only language model available for Turkish. We evaluate SindBERT on part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, offensive language detection, and the TurBLiMP linguistic acceptability benchmark. Our results show that SindBERT performs competitively with existing Turkish and multilingual models, with the large variant achieving the best scores in two of four tasks but showing no consistent scaling advantage overall. This flat scaling trend, also observed for XLM-R and EuroBERT, suggests that current Turkish benchmarks may already be saturated. At the same time, comparisons with smaller but more curated models such as BERTurk highlight that corpus quality and diversity can outweigh sheer data volume. Taken together, SindBERT contributes both as an openly released resource for Turkish NLP and as an empirical case study on the limits of scaling and the central role of corpus composition in morphologically rich languages. The SindBERT models are released under the MIT license and made available in both fairseq and Huggingface formats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at SIGTURK 2026, co-located with EACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:54:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Sample Complexity of Differentially Private Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:03:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T04:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T01:17:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:55:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:26:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Policy optimization (PO) is a cornerstone of modern reinforcement learning (RL), with diverse applications spanning robotics, healthcare, and large language model training. The increasing deployment of PO in sensitive domains, however, raises significant privacy concerns. In this paper, we initiate a theoretical study of differentially private policy optimization, focusing explicitly on its sample complexity. We first formalize an appropriate definition of differential privacy (DP) tailored to PO, addressing the inherent challenges arising from on-policy learning dynamics and the subtlety involved in defining the unit of privacy. We then systematically analyze the sample complexity of widely-used PO algorithms, including policy gradient (PG), natural policy gradient (NPG) and more, under DP constraints and various settings, via a unified framework. Our theoretical results demonstrate that privacy costs can often manifest as lower-order terms in the sample complexity, while also highlighting subtle yet important observations in private PO settings. These offer valuable practical insights for privacy-preserving PO algorithms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:48Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:50:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Kunz","name":"Kunz, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Diagnostic Benchmark for Sweden-Related Factual Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T14:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many Swedish benchmarks are translations of US-centric benchmarks and are therefore not suitable for testing knowledge that is particularly relevant, or even specific, to Sweden. We therefore introduce a manually written question-answering benchmark specifically targeted at Sweden-related personalities and events, many of which receive very limited coverage in international media. Our annotators drew inspiration from a popular radio program featuring public figures from culture and media, as well as major sports events in Sweden. The dataset can be used to measure factual recall across models of varying sizes and degrees of Swedish coverage, and allows probing of cross-lingual factual consistency, as it contains English translations. Using the dataset, we find that smaller models with stronger Swedish coverage perform comparably to a multilingual model three times larger in recalling Sweden-related facts. We also observe that continued pre-training on Swedish generally improves factual knowledge but leads to partial forgetting of previously known information. These results demonstrate the dataset's potential as a diagnostic tool for studying language adaptation and knowledge retention in multilingual models during language adaptation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:46:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21314","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21314","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21314","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jichu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jichu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Difan","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Difan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T05:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:01:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid scaling of large language models (LLMs) has made low-precision training essential for reducing memory, improving efficiency, and enabling larger models and datasets. Existing convergence theories for adaptive optimizers, however, assume all components are exact and neglect hardware-aware quantization, leaving open the question of why low-precision training remains effective. We introduce the first theoretical framework for analyzing the convergence of adaptive optimizers, including Adam and Muon, under floating-point quantization of gradients, weights, and optimizer states (e.g., moment estimates). Within this framework, we derive convergence rates on smooth non-convex objectives under standard stochastic gradient assumptions, explicitly characterizing how quantization errors from different components affect convergence. We show that both algorithms retain rates close to their full-precision counterparts provided mantissa length scales only logarithmically with the number of iterations. Our analysis further reveals that Adam is highly sensitive to weights and second-moment quantization due to its reliance on $β_2 \\to 1$, while Muon requires weaker error control and is thus potentially more robust. These results narrow the gap between empirical success and theoretical understanding of low-precision training methods. Numerical experiments on synthetic and real-world data corroborate our theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"68 pages, 13 figures, ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21314","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:48Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:46:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yarik Menchaca","familyName":"Resendiz","name":"Resendiz, Yarik Menchaca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Klinger","name":"Klinger, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PARL: Prompt-based Agents for Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T13:46:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated high performance on tasks expressed in natural language, particularly in zero- or few-shot settings. These are typically framed as supervised (e.g., classification) or unsupervised (e.g., clustering) problems. However, limited work evaluates LLMs as agents in reinforcement learning (RL) tasks (e.g., playing games), where learning occurs through interaction with an environment and a reward system. While prior work focused on representing tasks that rely on a language representation, we study structured, non-linguistic reasoning - such as interpreting positions in a grid world. We therefore introduce PARL (Prompt-based Agent for Reinforcement Learning), a method that uses LLMs as RL agents through prompting, without any fine-tuning. PARL encodes actions, states, and rewards in the prompt, enabling the model to learn through trial-and-error interaction. We evaluate PARL on three standard RL tasks that do not entirely rely on natural language. We show that it can match or outperform traditional RL agents in simple environments by leveraging pretrained knowledge. However, we identify performance limitations in tasks that require complex mathematical operations or decoding states and actions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:37Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:16:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael S.","familyName":"Albergo","name":"Albergo, Michael S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas M.","familyName":"Boffi","name":"Boffi, Nicholas M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael M.","familyName":"Bronstein","name":"Bronstein, Michael M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishek Joey","familyName":"Bose","name":"Bose, Avishek Joey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalised Flow Maps for Few-Step Generative Modelling on Riemannian Manifolds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:14:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T15:43:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:40:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Geometric data and purpose-built generative models on them have become ubiquitous in high-impact deep learning application domains, ranging from protein backbone generation and computational chemistry to geospatial data. Current geometric generative models remain computationally expensive at inference -- requiring many steps of complex numerical simulation -- as they are derived from dynamical measure transport frameworks such as diffusion and flow-matching on Riemannian manifolds. In this paper, we propose Generalised Flow Maps (GFM), a new class of few-step generative models that generalises the Flow Map framework in Euclidean spaces to arbitrary Riemannian manifolds. We instantiate GFMs with three self-distillation-based training methods: Generalised Lagrangian Flow Maps, Generalised Eulerian Flow Maps, and Generalised Progressive Flow Maps. We theoretically show that GFMs, under specific design decisions, unify and elevate existing Euclidean few-step generative models, such as consistency models, shortcut models, and meanflows, to the Riemannian setting. We benchmark GFMs against other geometric generative models on a suite of geometric datasets, including geospatial data, RNA torsion angles, and hyperbolic manifolds, and achieve state-of-the-art sample quality for single- and few-step evaluations, and superior or competitive log-likelihoods using the implicit probability flow."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:22:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxiang","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Wenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanting","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Guanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji-Rong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Ji-Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepAgent: A General Reasoning Agent with Scalable Toolsets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T08:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:33:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T14:08:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:49:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large reasoning models have demonstrated strong problem-solving abilities, yet real-world tasks often require external tools and long-horizon interactions. Existing agent frameworks typically follow predefined workflows, which limit autonomous and global task completion. In this paper, we introduce DeepAgent, an end-to-end deep reasoning agent that performs autonomous thinking, tool discovery, and action execution within a single, coherent reasoning process. To manage long-horizon interactions, we introduce an autonomous memory folding mechanism that compresses past interactions into structured episodic, working, and tool memories, reducing error accumulation while preserving critical information. To teach general-purpose tool use efficiently and stably, we develop an end-to-end reinforcement learning strategy, namely ToolPO, that leverages LLM-simulated APIs and applies tool-call advantage attribution to assign fine-grained credit to the tool invocation tokens. Extensive experiments on eight benchmarks, including general tool-use tasks (ToolBench, API-Bank, TMDB, Spotify, ToolHop) and downstream applications (ALFWorld, WebShop, GAIA, HLE), demonstrate that DeepAgent consistently outperforms baselines across both labeled-tool and open-set tool retrieval scenarios. The code and demo are available at https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/DeepAgent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sukanya","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Sukanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souhaib Ben","familyName":"Taieb","name":"Taieb, Souhaib Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evidence-Based Post-Hoc Adjustment Framework for Anomaly Detection Under Data Contamination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:38:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T19:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:04:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised anomaly detection (AD) methods typically assume clean training data, yet real-world datasets often contain undetected or mislabeled anomalies, leading to significant performance degradation. Existing solutions require access to the training pipelines, data or prior knowledge of the proportions of anomalies in the data, limiting their real-world applicability. To address this challenge, we propose EPHAD, a simple yet effective test-time adaptation framework that updates the outputs of AD models trained on contaminated datasets using evidence gathered at test time. Our approach integrates the prior knowledge captured by the AD model trained on contaminated datasets with evidence derived from multimodal foundation models like Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP), classical AD methods like the Local Outlier Factor or domain-specific knowledge. We illustrate the intuition behind EPHAD using a synthetic toy example and validate its effectiveness through comprehensive experiments across eight visual AD datasets, twenty-six tabular AD datasets, and a real-world industrial AD dataset. Additionally, we conduct an ablation study to analyse hyperparameter influence and robustness to varying contamination levels, demonstrating the versatility and robustness of EPHAD across diverse AD models and evidence pairs. To ensure reproducibility, our code is publicly available at https://github.com/sukanyapatra1997/EPHAD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in the Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Gaß","name":"Gaß, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harold C.","familyName":"Steinacker","name":"Steinacker, Harold C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatially flat cosmological quantum spacetimes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:27:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-24T07:39:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:20:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/6l9d-njcr","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We recently described a cosmological quantum spacetime of vanishing spatial curvature, which can be considered as background for the IKKT matrix model, assuming that the resulting gauge theory couples weakly. Building on this example, we construct a large class of spatially flat cosmological quantum spacetimes. We also elaborate on various details of their algebraic and semi-classical structure as well as the higher spin modes present in these models. In particular, we introduce the notion of approximate diffeomorphisms on the cosmological quantum spacetimes that stem from gauge transformations of the underlying matrix model, and investigate how different gauges are related in the semi-classical regime by approximate diffeomorphisms. Finally, we briefly outline how the described quantum spacetimes could be incorporated into the full IKKT model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: 39 pages, added an appendix on quantization"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiamang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weisi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weisi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Grouped Lattice Vector Quantizers for Low-Bit LLM Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:19:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:07:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T12:51:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities but typically require extensive computational resources and memory for inference. Post-training quantization (PTQ) can effectively reduce these demands by storing weights in lower bit-width formats. However, standard uniform quantization often leads to notable performance degradation, particularly in low-bit scenarios. In this work, we introduce a Grouped Lattice Vector Quantization (GLVQ) framework that assigns each group of weights a customized lattice codebook, defined by a learnable generation matrix. To address the non-differentiability of the quantization process, we adopt Babai rounding to approximate nearest-lattice-point search during training, which enables stable optimization of the generation matrices. Once trained, decoding reduces to a simple matrix-vector multiplication, yielding an efficient and practical quantization pipeline. Experiments on multiple benchmarks show that our approach achieves a better trade-off between model size and accuracy compared to existing post-training quantization baselines, highlighting its effectiveness in deploying large models under stringent resource constraints. Our source code is available on GitHub repository: https://github.com/xzhang9308/GLVQ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21153","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21153","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21153","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianghong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lianghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongkyu Eugene","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongkyu Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Domaratzki","name":"Domaratzki, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingzhao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Pingzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-Aware Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning-Guided Diffusion Models for 3D De Novo Molecular Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:49:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T04:12:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:16:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing de novo 3D molecules with desirable properties remains a fundamental challenge in drug discovery and molecular engineering. While diffusion models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in generating high-quality 3D molecular structures, they often struggle to effectively control complex multi-objective constraints critical for real-world applications. In this study, we propose an uncertainty-aware Reinforcement Learning (RL) framework to guide the optimization of 3D molecular diffusion models toward multiple property objectives while enhancing the overall quality of the generated molecules. Our method leverages surrogate models with predictive uncertainty estimation to dynamically shape reward functions, facilitating balance across multiple optimization objectives. We comprehensively evaluate our framework across three benchmark datasets and multiple diffusion model architectures, consistently outperforming baselines for molecular quality and property optimization. Additionally, Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulations and ADMET profiling of top generated candidates indicate promising drug-like behavior and binding stability, comparable to known Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR) inhibitors. Our results demonstrate the strong potential of RL-guided generative diffusion models for advancing automated molecular design."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21153","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:11:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huatian","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Huatian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuh-Biing","familyName":"Sheu","name":"Sheu, Jiuh-Biing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Model for Multi-Task Drone Routing in Post-Disaster Road Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T04:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:21:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-disaster road assessment (PDRA) is essential for emergency response, enabling rapid evaluation of infrastructure conditions and efficient allocation of resources. Although drones provide a flexible and effective tool for PDRA, routing them in large-scale networks remains challenging. Exact and heuristic optimization methods scale poorly and demand domain expertise, while existing deep reinforcement learning (DRL) approaches adopt a single-task paradigm, requiring separate models for each problem variant and lacking adaptability to evolving operational needs. This study proposes a unified model (UM) for drone routing that simultaneously addresses eight PDRA variants. By training a single neural network across multiple problem configurations, UM captures shared structural knowledge while adapting to variant-specific constraints through a modern transformer encoder-decoder architecture. A lightweight adapter mechanism further enables efficient finetuning to unseen attributes without retraining, enhancing deployment flexibility in dynamic disaster scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the UM reduces training time and parameters by a factor of eight compared with training separate models, while consistently outperforming single-task DRL methods by 6-14%, heuristic algorithms by 22-42%, and commercial solvers by 24-82% in terms of solution quality (total collected information value). The model achieves rapid solutions (1-10 seconds) across networks of up to 1,000 nodes, with robustness confirmed through sensitivity analyses. Moreover, finetuning experiments show that unseen attributes can be effectively incorporated with minimal cost while retaining high solution quality. The source code for UM is publicly available at https://github.com/PJ-HTU/UM_PDRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 8 figures,9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:42Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:58:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Science Institute and Faculty of Physical Sciences, University of Iceland, Reykjavík, Iceland"],"givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Rohit","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Exploration of Chemical Kinetics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Atomic Physics (physics.atom-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T17:13:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:53:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Estimating reaction rates and chemical stability is fundamental, yet efficient methods for large-scale simulations remain out of reach despite advances in modeling and exascale computing. Direct simulation is limited by short timescales; machine-learned potentials require large data sets and struggle with transition state regions essential for reaction rates. Reaction network exploration with sufficient accuracy is hampered by the computational cost of electronic structure calculations, and even simplifications like harmonic transition state theory rely on prohibitively expensive saddle point searches. Surrogate model-based acceleration has been promising but hampered by overhead and numerical instability.\n This dissertation presents a holistic solution, co-designing physical representations, statistical models, and systems architecture in the Optimal Transport Gaussian Process (OT-GP) framework. Using physics-aware optimal transport metrics, OT-GP creates compact, chemically relevant surrogates of the potential energy surface, underpinned by statistically robust sampling. Alongside EON software rewrites for long timescale simulations, we introduce reinforcement learning approaches for both minimum-mode following (when the final state is unknown) and nudged elastic band methods (when endpoints are specified). Collectively, these advances establish a representation-first, modular approach to chemical kinetics simulation. Large-scale benchmarks and Bayesian hierarchical validation demonstrate state-of-the-art performance and practical exploration of chemical kinetics, transforming a longstanding theoretical promise into a working engine for discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Doctoral dissertation, 121 pages, ISBN: 978-9935-9826-5-0. By design, all text and figures within this thesis are original and do not appear in the associated papers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:58:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Bezick","name":"Bezick, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vittorio","familyName":"Giammarino","name":"Giammarino, Vittorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed H.","familyName":"Qureshi","name":"Qureshi, Ahmed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Point Cloud Reinforcement Learning via PCA-Based Canonicalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:06:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T11:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:48:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) from raw visual input has achieved impressive successes in recent years, yet it remains fragile to out-of-distribution variations such as changes in lighting, color, and viewpoint. Point Cloud Reinforcement Learning (PC-RL) offers a promising alternative by mitigating appearance-based brittleness, but its sensitivity to camera pose mismatches continues to undermine reliability in realistic settings. To address this challenge, we propose PCA Point Cloud (PPC), a canonicalization framework specifically tailored for downstream robotic control. PPC maps point clouds under arbitrary rigid-body transformations to a unique canonical pose, aligning observations to a consistent frame, thereby substantially decreasing viewpoint-induced inconsistencies. In our experiments, we show that PPC improves robustness to unseen camera poses across challenging robotic tasks, providing a principled alternative to domain randomization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:24:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuganjing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wuganjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhou","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhenzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaolong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Qiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongduo","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Yongduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-turn Training with Basic Human Feedback Helps Little on LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:08:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T11:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:37:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) are typically developed through the single-turn reinforcement learning, whereas real-world applications often involve multi-turn interactions with human feedback, leading to a potential mismatch between training and deployment conditions. In this work, we study whether multi-turn training with human feedback is necessary for reasoning tasks. We compare conventional single-turn training with three multi-turn strategies and reach contrary conclusions to previous research. We find that models trained in a single-turn setting generalize effectively to both single- and multi-turn evaluations, while models trained with multi-turn strategies exhibit a significant degradation in single-turn reasoning performance. These results suggest that for tasks with complete information, robust single-turn training remains more effective and reliable, as multi-turn training with basic feedback provides limited benefits and can even degrade reasoning capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:24Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hanqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianfan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Tianfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangru","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xiangru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng-Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng-Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RiboPO: Preference Optimization for Structure- and Stability-Aware RNA Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:25:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:16:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing RNA sequences that reliably adopt specified three-dimensional structures while maintaining thermodynamic stability remains challenging for synthetic biology and therapeutics. Current inverse folding approaches optimize for sequence recovery or single structural metrics, failing to simultaneously ensure global geometry, local accuracy, and ensemble stability-three interdependent requirements for functional RNA design. This gap becomes critical when designed sequences encounter dynamic biological environments. We introduce RiboPO, a Ribonucleic acid Preference Optimization framework that addresses this multi-objective challenge through reinforcement learning from physical feedback (RLPF). RiboPO fine-tunes gRNAde by constructing preference pairs from composite physical criteria that couple global 3D fidelity and thermodynamic stability. Preferences are formed using structural gates, PLDDT geometry assessments, and thermostability proxies with variability-aware margins, and the policy is updated with Direct Preference Optimization (DPO). On RNA inverse folding benchmarks, RiboPO demonstrates a superior balance of structural accuracy and stability. Compared to the best non-overlap baselines, our multi-round model improves Minimum Free Energy (MFE) by 12.3% and increases secondary-structure self-consistency (EternaFold scMCC) by 20%, while maintaining competitive 3D quality and high sequence diversity. In sampling efficiency, RiboPO achieves 11% higher pass@64 than the gRNAde base under the conjunction of multiple requirements. A multi-round variant with preference-pair reconstruction delivers additional gains on unseen RNA structures. These results establish RLPF as an effective paradigm for structure-accurate and ensemble-robust RNA design, providing a foundation for extending to complex biological objectives."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 2 figures. Equal contribution: Minghao Sun, Hanqun Cao, Zhou Zhang. Corresponding author: Fang Wu, Yang Zhang"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:11Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reuben","familyName":"Narad","name":"Narad, Reuben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonard","familyName":"Boussioux","name":"Boussioux, Leonard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural TSP Solvers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:54:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural networks have advanced combinatorial optimization, with Transformer-based solvers achieving near-optimal solutions on the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) in milliseconds. However, these models operate as black boxes, providing no insight into the geometric patterns they learn or the heuristics they employ during tour construction. We address this opacity by applying sparse autoencoders (SAEs), a mechanistic interpretability technique, to a Transformer-based TSP solver, representing the first application of activation-based interpretability methods to operations research models. We train a pointer network with reinforcement learning on 100-node instances, then fit an SAE to the encoder's residual stream to discover an overcomplete dictionary of interpretable features. Our analysis reveals that the solver naturally develops features mirroring fundamental TSP concepts: boundary detectors that activate on convex-hull nodes, cluster-sensitive features responding to locally dense regions, and separator features encoding geometric partitions. These findings provide the first model-internal account of what neural TSP solvers compute before node selection, demonstrate that geometric structure emerges without explicit supervision, and suggest pathways toward transparent hybrid systems that combine neural efficiency with algorithmic interpretability. Interactive feature explorer: https://reubennarad.github.io/TSP_interp"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:34Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21649","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21649","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21649","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangjun","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Guangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Dynamic Knowledge Distillation Method Based on the Gompertz Curve"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:07:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces a novel dynamic knowledge distillation framework, Gompertz-CNN, which integrates the Gompertz growth model into the training process to address the limitations of traditional knowledge distillation. Conventional methods often fail to capture the evolving cognitive capacity of student models, leading to suboptimal knowledge transfer. To overcome this, we propose a stage-aware distillation strategy that dynamically adjusts the weight of distillation loss based on the Gompertz curve, reflecting the student's learning progression: slow initial growth, rapid mid-phase improvement, and late-stage saturation. Our framework incorporates Wasserstein distance to measure feature-level discrepancies and gradient matching to align backward propagation behaviors between teacher and student models. These components are unified under a multi-loss objective, where the Gompertz curve modulates the influence of distillation losses over time. Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 using various teacher-student architectures (e.g., ResNet50 and MobileNet_v2) demonstrate that Gompertz-CNN consistently outperforms traditional distillation methods, achieving up to 8% and 4% accuracy gains on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, respectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21649","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tala","familyName":"Aljaafari","name":"Aljaafari, Tala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Kanade","name":"Kanade, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian Schroeder","familyName":"de Witt","name":"de Witt, Christian Schroeder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DEEDEE: Fast and Scalable Out-of-Distribution Dynamics Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:51:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deploying reinforcement learning (RL) in safety-critical settings is constrained by brittleness under distribution shift. We study out-of-distribution (OOD) detection for RL time series and introduce DEEDEE, a two-statistic detector that revisits representation-heavy pipelines with a minimal alternative. DEEDEE uses only an episodewise mean and an RBF kernel similarity to a training summary, capturing complementary global and local deviations. Despite its simplicity, DEEDEE matches or surpasses contemporary detectors across standard RL OOD suites, delivering a 600-fold reduction in compute (FLOPs / wall-time) and an average 5% absolute accuracy gain over strong baselines. Conceptually, our results indicate that diverse anomaly types often imprint on RL trajectories through a small set of low-order statistics, suggesting a compact foundation for OOD detection in complex environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiufu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiufu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linlin","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Linlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DAP-MAE: Domain-Adaptive Point Cloud Masked Autoencoder for Effective Cross-Domain Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compared to 2D data, the scale of point cloud data in different domains available for training, is quite limited. Researchers have been trying to combine these data of different domains for masked autoencoder (MAE) pre-training to leverage such a data scarcity issue. However, the prior knowledge learned from mixed domains may not align well with the downstream 3D point cloud analysis tasks, leading to degraded performance. To address such an issue, we propose the Domain-Adaptive Point Cloud Masked Autoencoder (DAP-MAE), an MAE pre-training method, to adaptively integrate the knowledge of cross-domain datasets for general point cloud analysis. In DAP-MAE, we design a heterogeneous domain adapter that utilizes an adaptation mode during pre-training, enabling the model to comprehensively learn information from point clouds across different domains, while employing a fusion mode in the fine-tuning to enhance point cloud features. Meanwhile, DAP-MAE incorporates a domain feature generator to guide the adaptation of point cloud features to various downstream tasks. With only one pre-training, DAP-MAE achieves excellent performance across four different point cloud analysis tasks, reaching 95.18% in object classification on ScanObjectNN and 88.45% in facial expression recognition on Bosphorus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures, conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:13Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Hamman","name":"Hamman, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pasan","familyName":"Dissanayake","name":"Dissanayake, Pasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghamitra","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sanghamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few-Shot Knowledge Distillation of LLMs With Counterfactual Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:36:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge distillation is a promising approach to transfer capabilities from complex teacher models to smaller, resource-efficient student models that can be deployed easily, particularly in task-aware scenarios. However, existing methods of task-aware distillation typically require substantial quantities of data which may be unavailable or expensive to obtain in many practical scenarios. In this paper, we address this challenge by introducing a novel strategy called Counterfactual-explanation-infused Distillation CoD for few-shot task-aware knowledge distillation by systematically infusing counterfactual explanations. Counterfactual explanations (CFEs) refer to inputs that can flip the output prediction of the teacher model with minimum perturbation. Our strategy CoD leverages these CFEs to precisely map the teacher's decision boundary with significantly fewer samples. We provide theoretical guarantees for motivating the role of CFEs in distillation, from both statistical and geometric perspectives. We mathematically show that CFEs can improve parameter estimation by providing more informative examples near the teacher's decision boundary. We also derive geometric insights on how CFEs effectively act as knowledge probes, helping the students mimic the teacher's decision boundaries more effectively than standard data. We perform experiments across various datasets and LLMs to show that CoD outperforms standard distillation approaches in few-shot regimes (as low as 8-512 samples). Notably, CoD only uses half of the original samples used by the baselines, paired with their corresponding CFEs and still improves performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elle","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Elle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trevor","familyName":"McInroe","name":"McInroe, Trevor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Abel","name":"Abel, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oisin","familyName":"Mac Aodha","name":"Mac Aodha, Oisin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sethu","familyName":"Vijayakumar","name":"Vijayakumar, Sethu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Tactile-based Reinforcement Learning for Robotic Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:15:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Achieving safe, reliable real-world robotic manipulation requires agents to evolve beyond vision and incorporate tactile sensing to overcome sensory deficits and reliance on idealised state information. Despite its potential, the efficacy of tactile sensing in reinforcement learning (RL) remains inconsistent. We address this by developing self-supervised learning (SSL) methodologies to more effectively harness tactile observations, focusing on a scalable setup of proprioception and sparse binary contacts. We empirically demonstrate that sparse binary tactile signals are critical for dexterity, particularly for interactions that proprioceptive control errors do not register, such as decoupled robot-object motions. Our agents achieve superhuman dexterity in complex contact tasks (ball bouncing and Baoding ball rotation). Furthermore, we find that decoupling the SSL memory from the on-policy memory can improve performance. We release the Robot Tactile Olympiad (RoTO) benchmark to standardise and promote future research in tactile-based manipulation. Project page: https://elle-miller.github.io/tactile_rl"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:37Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Joey Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuozhu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuozhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modest-Align: Data-Efficient Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-modal alignment aims to map heterogeneous modalities into a shared latent space, as exemplified by models like CLIP, which benefit from large-scale image-text pretraining for strong recognition capabilities. However, when operating in resource-constrained settings with limited or low-quality data, these models often suffer from overconfidence and degraded performance due to the prevalence of ambiguous or weakly correlated image-text pairs. Current contrastive learning approaches, which rely on single positive pairs, further exacerbate this issue by reinforcing overconfidence on uncertain samples. To address these challenges, we propose Modest-Align, a lightweight alignment framework designed for robustness and efficiency. Our approach leverages two complementary strategies -- Random Perturbation, which introduces controlled noise to simulate uncertainty, and Embedding Smoothing, which calibrates similarity distributions in the embedding space. These mechanisms collectively reduce overconfidence and improve performance on noisy or weakly aligned samples. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmark datasets demonstrate that Modest-Align outperforms state-of-the-art methods in retrieval tasks, achieving competitive results with over 100x less training data and 600x less GPU time than CLIP. Our method offers a practical and scalable solution for cross-modal alignment in real-world, low-resource scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xueyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cehao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjunchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rongjunchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengjin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chengjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RETuning: Upgrading Inference-Time Scaling for Stock Movement Prediction with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated outstanding reasoning capabilities on mathematical and coding tasks. However, their application to financial tasks-especially the most fundamental task of stock movement prediction-remains underexplored. We study a three-class classification problem (up, hold, down) and, by analyzing existing reasoning responses, observe that: (1) LLMs follow analysts' opinions rather than exhibit a systematic, independent analytical logic (CoTs). (2) LLMs list summaries from different sources without weighing adversarial evidence, yet such counterevidence is crucial for reliable prediction. It shows that the model does not make good use of its reasoning ability to complete the task. To address this, we propose Reflective Evidence Tuning (RETuning), a cold-start method prior to reinforcement learning, to enhance prediction ability. While generating CoT, RETuning encourages dynamically constructing an analytical framework from diverse information sources, organizing and scoring evidence for price up or down based on that framework-rather than on contextual viewpoints-and finally reflecting to derive the prediction. This approach maximally aligns the model with its learned analytical framework, ensuring independent logical reasoning and reducing undue influence from context. We also build a large-scale dataset spanning all of 2024 for 5,123 A-share stocks, with long contexts (32K tokens) and over 200K samples. In addition to price and news, it incorporates analysts' opinions, quantitative reports, fundamental data, macroeconomic indicators, and similar stocks. Experiments show that RETuning successfully unlocks the model's reasoning ability in the financial domain. Inference-time scaling still works even after 6 months or on out-of-distribution stocks, since the models gain valuable insights about stock movement prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José","familyName":"Rojas","name":"Rojas, José","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique","familyName":"Casanova","name":"Casanova, Enrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melvin","familyName":"Arias","name":"Arias, Melvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Schrödinger and Carroll Schrödinger Equations: Dualities and Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:02:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate precise structural relations between the standard Schrödinger equation and its Carrollian analogue-the Carroll-Schrödinger equation-in 1+1 dimensions, with emphasis on dualities, potential maps, and solution behavior. Our contributions proceed in the order of the paper: (i) we encode both dynamics with operators $H$ and $F$ under external potentials and explore conditions for obtaining the same type of solutions within both formalisms; (ii) we construct a potential-dependent reparametrization $x = δ(t)$ mapping the space-independent Carroll equation to the time-independent Schrödinger equation, and derive a Schwarzian relation that specifies the map $δ$ for any static $V_{sch}$ (with harmonic, Coulomb-like, and free examples); (iii) we relate conserved densities and currents by removing $V_{car}$ through a gauge transform followed by a coordinate inversion, establishing equivalence of the continuity equations; (iv) we obtain a Carrollian dispersion relation from an ultra-boost of the energy-momentum two-vector and also derive the classical limit of the Carroll wave equation via the Hamilton-Jacobi formalism; (v) we place Carroll dynamics on an equal-$x$ Hilbert space $L^2(R_t)$, prove unitary $x$-evolution, and illustrate dynamics with an exactly solvable Gaussian packet and finite-time quantization for time-localized perturbations; and (vi) for general $V(x; t)$ we perform a gauge reduction to an interaction momentum and set up a controlled Dyson expansion about solvable time profiles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:21Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yassine El","familyName":"Ouahidi","name":"Ouahidi, Yassine El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Lys","name":"Lys, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Thölke","name":"Thölke, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Farrugia","name":"Farrugia, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bastien","familyName":"Pasdeloup","name":"Pasdeloup, Bastien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Gripon","name":"Gripon, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim","familyName":"Jerbi","name":"Jerbi, Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Lioi","name":"Lioi, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REVE: A Foundation Model for EEG -- Adapting to Any Setup with Large-Scale Pretraining on 25,000 Subjects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:52:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have transformed AI by reducing reliance on task-specific data through large-scale pretraining. While successful in language and vision, their adoption in EEG has lagged due to the heterogeneity of public datasets, which are collected under varying protocols, devices, and electrode configurations. Existing EEG foundation models struggle to generalize across these variations, often restricting pretraining to a single setup, resulting in suboptimal performance, in particular under linear probing. We present REVE (Representation for EEG with Versatile Embeddings), a pretrained model explicitly designed to generalize across diverse EEG signals. REVE introduces a novel 4D positional encoding scheme that enables it to process signals of arbitrary length and electrode arrangement. Using a masked autoencoding objective, we pretrain REVE on over 60,000 hours of EEG data from 92 datasets spanning 25,000 subjects, representing the largest EEG pretraining effort to date. REVE achieves state-of-the-art results on 10 downstream EEG tasks, including motor imagery classification, seizure detection, sleep staging, cognitive load estimation, and emotion recognition. With little to no fine-tuning, it demonstrates strong generalization, and nuanced spatio-temporal modeling. We release code, pretrained weights, and tutorials to support standardized EEG research and accelerate progress in clinical neuroscience."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at: https://brain-bzh.github.io/reve/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:04Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petar","familyName":"Veličković","name":"Veličković, Petar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Classical Algorithms for Graph Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:42:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural networks excel at processing unstructured data but often fail to generalise out-of-distribution, whereas classical algorithms guarantee correctness but lack flexibility. We explore whether pretraining Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) on classical algorithms can improve their performance on molecular property prediction tasks from the Open Graph Benchmark: ogbg-molhiv (HIV inhibition) and ogbg-molclintox (clinical toxicity). GNNs trained on 24 classical algorithms from the CLRS Algorithmic Reasoning Benchmark are used to initialise and freeze selected layers of a second GNN for molecular prediction. Compared to a randomly initialised baseline, the pretrained models achieve consistent wins or ties, with the Segments Intersect algorithm pretraining yielding a 6% absolute gain on ogbg-molhiv and Dijkstra pretraining achieving a 3% gain on ogbg-molclintox. These results demonstrate embedding classical algorithmic priors into GNNs provides useful inductive biases, boosting performance on complex, real-world graph data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengtang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chengtang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingqi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizhi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lily","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaolong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baining","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Baining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Vision-Language-Action Model Pretraining for Robotic Manipulation with Real-Life Human Activity Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel approach for pretraining robotic manipulation Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models using a large corpus of unscripted real-life video recordings of human hand activities. Treating human hand as dexterous robot end-effector, we show that \"in-the-wild\" egocentric human videos without any annotations can be transformed into data formats fully aligned with existing robotic V-L-A training data in terms of task granularity and labels. This is achieved by the development of a fully-automated holistic human activity analysis approach for arbitrary human hand videos. This approach can generate atomic-level hand activity segments and their language descriptions, each accompanied with framewise 3D hand motion and camera motion. We process a large volume of egocentric videos and create a hand-VLA training dataset containing 1M episodes and 26M frames. This training data covers a wide range of objects and concepts, dexterous manipulation tasks, and environment variations in real life, vastly exceeding the coverage of existing robot data. We design a dexterous hand VLA model architecture and pretrain the model on this dataset. The model exhibits strong zero-shot capabilities on completely unseen real-world observations. Additionally, fine-tuning it on a small amount of real robot action data significantly improves task success rates and generalization to novel objects in real robotic experiments. We also demonstrate the appealing scaling behavior of the model's task performance with respect to pretraining data scale. We believe this work lays a solid foundation for scalable VLA pretraining, advancing robots toward truly generalizable embodied intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://microsoft.github.io/VITRA/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:45Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junni","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Junni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GranViT: A Fine-Grained Vision Model With Autoregressive Perception For MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:33:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision encoders are indispensable for allowing impressive performance of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) in vision language tasks such as visual question answering and reasoning. However, existing vision encoders focus on global image representations but overlook fine-grained regional analysis. They are limited in fine grained perception due to the scarcity of fine grained annotated data and the lack of a fine grained pre-training paradigm. In this paper, we propose GranViT, a novel Vision Transformer that integrates fine-grained feature extraction with semantic alignment to Large Language Models (LLMs) via region level autoregressive training. We first construct Gran-29M, a dataset comprising 2million natural and OCR images paired with over 180 million high-quality region-level annotations, to enable large scale fine grained pretraining. Consequently, we develop a pretraining-adaptation framework along with a self distillation mechanism to train fine-grained GranViT on Gran-29M. We sufficiently exploit the fine-grained annotations from Gran-29M to resort to bounding-box-to-caption regression to enhance localized visual representation of the vision encoder in the pretraining and caption-to-bounding-box regression to improve vision feature utilization and localization for LLM in the adaptation. We further incorporate a self distillation mechanism that imposes explicit localization constraints on the vision encoder to strengthen its regional reasoning capability. Extensive experiments show that GranViT surpasses existing vision encoders and attains strong transferability to varying LLMs. Remarkably, it achieves state-of-the-art results on fine-grained recognition, multimodal VQA, and OCR understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenglong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Murun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chunliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MRO: Enhancing Reasoning in Diffusion Language Models via Multi-Reward Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in diffusion language models (DLMs) have presented a promising alternative to traditional autoregressive large language models (LLMs). However, DLMs still lag behind LLMs in reasoning performance, especially as the number of denoising steps decreases. Our analysis reveals that this shortcoming arises primarily from the independent generation of masked tokens across denoising steps, which fails to capture the token correlation. In this paper, we define two types of token correlation: intra-sequence correlation and inter-sequence correlation, and demonstrate that enhancing these correlations improves reasoning performance. To this end, we propose a Multi-Reward Optimization (MRO) approach, which encourages DLMs to consider the token correlation during the denoising process. More specifically, our MRO approach leverages test-time scaling, reject sampling, and reinforcement learning to directly optimize the token correlation with multiple elaborate rewards. Additionally, we introduce group step and importance sampling strategies to mitigate reward variance and enhance sampling efficiency. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that MRO not only improves reasoning performance but also achieves significant sampling speedups while maintaining high performance on reasoning benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuojing","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zhuojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified token representations for sequential decision models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:25:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have demonstrated strong potential in offline reinforcement learning (RL) by modeling trajectories as sequences of return-to-go, states, and actions. However, existing approaches such as the Decision Transformer(DT) and its variants suffer from redundant tokenization and quadratic attention complexity, limiting their scalability in real-time or resource-constrained settings. To address this, we propose a Unified Token Representation (UTR) that merges return-to-go, state, and action into a single token, substantially reducing sequence length and model complexity. Theoretical analysis shows that UTR leads to a tighter Rademacher complexity bound, suggesting improved generalization. We further develop two variants: UDT and UDC, built upon transformer and gated CNN backbones, respectively. Both achieve comparable or superior performance to state-of-the-art methods with markedly lower computation. These findings demonstrate that UTR generalizes well across architectures and may provide an efficient foundation for scalable control in future large decision models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21427","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21427","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21427","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuqing","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Shuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yudi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Biwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yali","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Causality Meets Locality: Provably Generalizable and Scalable Policy Learning for Networked Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:06:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale networked systems, such as traffic, power, and wireless grids, challenge reinforcement-learning agents with both scale and environment shifts. To address these challenges, we propose GSAC (Generalizable and Scalable Actor-Critic), a framework that couples causal representation learning with meta actor-critic learning to achieve both scalability and domain generalization. Each agent first learns a sparse local causal mask that provably identifies the minimal neighborhood variables influencing its dynamics, yielding exponentially tight approximately compact representations (ACRs) of state and domain factors. These ACRs bound the error of truncating value functions to $κ$-hop neighborhoods, enabling efficient learning on graphs. A meta actor-critic then trains a shared policy across multiple source domains while conditioning on the compact domain factors; at test time, a few trajectories suffice to estimate the new domain factor and deploy the adapted policy. We establish finite-sample guarantees on causal recovery, actor-critic convergence, and adaptation gap, and show that GSAC adapts rapidly and significantly outperforms learning-from-scratch and conventional adaptation baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 (Spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21427","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:27Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravindra Aribowo","familyName":"Tarunokusumo","name":"Tarunokusumo, Ravindra Aribowo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael Fernandes","familyName":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Rafael Fernandes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boosting Accuracy and Efficiency of Budget Forcing in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning for Mathematical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:39:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling methods have seen a rapid increase in popularity for its computational efficiency and parameter-independent training to improve reasoning performance on Large Language Models. One such method is called budget forcing, a decoding intervention strategy which allocates extra compute budget for thinking and elicits the inherent self-correcting behavior of the model. However, this relies on supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on long-context reasoning traces which causes performance degradation on smaller models due to verbose responses. For this reason, we offer a framework integrating reinforcement learning (RL) to improve token efficiency and boost the performance of a 1.5B model for mathematical reasoning. We demonstrate this using only 1.5K training samples and found that our SFT+RL model performed better on the GSM8K dataset with varying compute budgets. Our main findings showed an overall higher accuracy while significantly reducing its token usage by over 40% compared to the SFT model, revealing how RL can recover the losses due to long-context training and altogether improving performance in mathematical reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to the European Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ECAI)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21388","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21388","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21388","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshdeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Arshdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinayak","familyName":"Abrol","name":"Abrol, Vinayak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark D.","familyName":"Plumbley","name":"Plumbley, Mark D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compressing Quaternion Convolutional Neural Networks for Audio Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:46:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) in the real domain have been widely used for audio classification. However, their convolution operations process multi-channel inputs independently, limiting the ability to capture correlations among channels. This can lead to suboptimal feature learning, particularly for complex audio patterns such as multi-channel spectrogram representations. Quaternion Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) address this limitation by employing quaternion algebra to jointly capture inter-channel dependencies, enabling more compact models with fewer learnable parameters while better exploiting the multi-dimensional nature of audio signals. However, QCNNs exhibit higher computational complexity due to the overhead of quaternion operations, resulting in increased inference latency and reduced efficiency compared to conventional CNNs, posing challenges for deployment on resource-constrained platforms. To address this challenge, this study explores knowledge distillation (KD) and pruning, to reduce the computational complexity of QCNNs while maintaining performance. Our experiments on audio classification reveal that pruning QCNNs achieves similar or superior performance compared to KD while requiring less computational effort. Compared to conventional CNNs and Transformer-based architectures, pruned QCNNs achieve competitive performance with a reduced learnable parameter count and computational complexity. On the AudioSet dataset, pruned QCNNs reduce computational cost by 50\\% and parameter count by 80\\%, while maintaining performance comparable to the conventional CNNs. Furthermore, pruned QCNNs generalize well across multiple audio classification benchmarks, including GTZAN for music genre recognition, ESC-50 for environmental sound classification and RAVDESS for speech emotion recognition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review in IEEE TASLPRO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21388","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Lopez-Montes","name":"Lopez-Montes, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Seifert","name":"Seifert, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Astrid","familyName":"Delker","name":"Delker, Astrid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Boening","name":"Boening, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Clement","name":"Clement, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Afshar-Oromieh","name":"Afshar-Oromieh, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Rominger","name":"Rominger, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuangyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Kuangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Patient-specific AI for generation of 3D dosimetry imaging from two 2D-planar measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Medical Physics (physics.med-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work we explored the use of patient specific reinforced learning to generate 3D activity maps from two 2D planar images (anterior and posterior). The solution of this problem remains unachievable using conventional methodologies and is of particular interest for dosimetry in nuclear medicine where approaches for post-therapy distribution of radiopharmaceuticals such as 177Lu-PSMA are typically done via either expensive and long 3D SPECT acquisitions or fast, yet only 2D, planar scintigraphy. Being able to generate 3D activity maps from planar scintigraphy opens the gate for new dosimetry applications removing the need for SPECT and facilitating multi-time point dosimetry studies. Our solution comprises the generation of a patient specific dataset with possible 3D uptake maps of the radiopharmaceuticals withing the anatomy of the individual followed by an AI approach (we explored both the use of 3DUnet and diffusion models) able to generate 3D activity maps from 2D planar images. We have validated our method both in simulation and real planar acquisitions. We observed enhanced results using patient specific reinforcement learning (~20% reduction on MAE and ~5% increase in SSIM) and better organ delineation and patient anatomy especially when combining diffusion models with patient specific training yielding a SSIM=0.89 compared to the ground truth for simulations and 0.73 when compared to a SPECT acquisition performed half an hour after the planar. We believe that our methodology can set a change of paradigm for nuclear medicine dosimetry allowing for 3D quantification using only planar scintigraphy without the need of expensive and time-consuming SPECT leveraging the pre-therapy information of the patients."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at IEEE NSS/MIC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:56Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazuo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiazuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haomiao","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Haomiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huchuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"He","name":"He, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FineRS: Fine-grained Reasoning and Segmentation of Small Objects with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:14:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities across a wide range of vision-language tasks. However, due to the restricted input resolutions, MLLMs face significant challenges in precisely understanding and localizing visual details in high-resolution images -- particularly when dealing with extra-small objects embedded in cluttered contexts. To address this issue, we propose \\textsc{FineRS}, a two-stage MLLM-based reinforcement learning framework for jointly reasoning and segmenting extremely small objects within high-resolution scenes. \\textsc{FineRS} adopts a coarse-to-fine pipeline comprising Global Semantic Exploration (GSE) and Localized Perceptual Refinement (LPR). Specifically, GSE performs instruction-guided reasoning to generate a textural response and a coarse target region, while LPR refines this region to produce an accurate bounding box and segmentation mask. To couple the two stages, we introduce a locate-informed retrospective reward, where LPR's outputs are used to optimize GSE for more robust coarse region exploration. % Additionally, we present \\textsc{FineRS}-4k, a new dataset for evaluating MLLMs on attribute-level reasoning and pixel-level segmentation on subtle, small-scale targets in complex high-resolution scenes. Experimental results on \\textsc{FineRS}-4k and public datasets demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MLLM-based approaches on both instruction-guided segmentation and visual reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumiko","familyName":"Tanaka-Ishii","name":"Tanaka-Ishii, Kumiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correlation Dimension of Auto-Regressive Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:35:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in natural language generation, yet they continue to display puzzling behaviors -- such as repetition and incoherence -- even when exhibiting low perplexity. This highlights a key limitation of conventional evaluation metrics, which emphasize local prediction accuracy while overlooking long-range structural complexity. We introduce correlation dimension, a fractal-geometric measure of self-similarity, to quantify the epistemological complexity of text as perceived by a language model. This measure captures the hierarchical recurrence structure of language, bridging local and global properties in a unified framework. Through extensive experiments, we show that correlation dimension (1) reveals three distinct phases during pretraining, (2) reflects context-dependent complexity, (3) indicates a model's tendency toward hallucination, and (4) reliably detects multiple forms of degeneration in generated text. The method is computationally efficient, robust to model quantization (down to 4-bit precision), broadly applicable across autoregressive architectures (e.g., Transformer and Mamba), and provides fresh insight into the generative dynamics of LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Scool, CRIStAL"],"givenName":"Waris","familyName":"Radji","name":"Radji, Waris","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Scool, CRIStAL"],"givenName":"Odalric-Ambrym","familyName":"Maillard","name":"Maillard, Odalric-Ambrym","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Hard is it to Confuse a World Model?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:08:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In reinforcement learning (RL) theory, the concept of most confusing instances is central to establishing regret lower bounds, that is, the minimal exploration needed to solve a problem. Given a reference model and its optimal policy, a most confusing instance is the statistically closest alternative model that makes a suboptimal policy optimal. While this concept is well-studied in multi-armed bandits and ergodic tabular Markov decision processes, constructing such instances remains an open question in the general case. In this paper, we formalize this problem for neural network world models as a constrained optimization: finding a modified model that is statistically close to the reference one, while producing divergent performance between optimal and suboptimal policies. We propose an adversarial training procedure to solve this problem and conduct an empirical study across world models of varying quality. Our results suggest that the degree of achievable confusion correlates with uncertainty in the approximate model, which may inform theoretically-grounded exploration strategies for deep model-based RL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Kexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yandong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Model Merging with Functional Dual Anchors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:54:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model merging is an efficient post-training strategy for integrating knowledge from multiple finetuned checkpoints of a shared foundation model. Existing methods operate in the parameter space, combining task vectors to mitigate conflicts, but remain constrained by parameter inconsistencies. We propose Functional Dual Anchors (FDAs), a framework that instead models the input-representation space. FDAs are synthetic inputs whose induced gradients align with task vectors, capturing task-specific functional shifts relative to the pretrained model. This perspective bridges joint multi-task training and post-hoc merging, offering both robustness and flexibility. We further introduce a principled initialization scheme and show that FDAs are complementary to parameter-space model merging. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of FDAs in model merging."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report (23 pages, 15 figures, project page: https://spherelab.ai/fda/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Brekelmans","name":"Brekelmans, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Grosse","name":"Grosse, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reducing the Probability of Undesirable Outputs in Language Models Using Probabilistic Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:23:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a predominant technique to align language models (LMs) with human preferences or promote outputs which are deemed to be desirable by a given reward function. Standard RL approaches optimize average reward, while methods explicitly focused on reducing the probability of undesired outputs typically come at a cost to average-case performance. To improve this tradeoff, we introduce RePULSe, a new training method that augments the standard RL loss with an additional loss that uses learned proposals to guide sampling low-reward outputs, and then reduces those outputs' probability. We run experiments demonstrating that RePULSe produces a better tradeoff of expected reward versus the probability of undesired outputs and is more adversarially robust, compared to standard RL alignment approaches and alternatives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:43Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chujun","familyName":"He","name":"He, Chujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhonghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical AI Multi-Agent Fundamental Investing: Evidence from China's A-Share Market"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Portfolio Management (q-fin.PM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:38:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a multi-agent, AI-driven framework for fundamental investing that integrates macro indicators, industry-level and firm-specific information to construct optimized equity portfolios. The architecture comprises: (i) a Macro agent that dynamically screens and weights sectors based on evolving economic indicators and industry performance; (ii) four firm-level agents -- Fundamental, Technical, Report, and News -- that conduct in-depth analyses of individual firms to ensure both breadth and depth of coverage; (iii) a Portfolio agent that uses reinforcement learning to combine the agent outputs into a unified policy to generate the trading strategy; and (iv) a Risk Control agent that adjusts portfolio positions in response to market volatility. We evaluate the system on the constituents by the CSI 300 Index of China's A-share market and find that it consistently outperforms standard benchmarks and a state-of-the-art multi-agent trading system on risk-adjusted returns and drawdown control. Our core contribution is a hierarchical multi-agent design that links top-down macro screening with bottom-up fundamental analysis, offering a robust and extensible approach to factor-based portfolio construction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxin","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Guangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Meet Text-Attributed Graphs: A Survey of Integration Frameworks and Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:53:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:22:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in natural language processing through strong semantic understanding and generation. However, their black-box nature limits structured and multi-hop reasoning. In contrast, Text-Attributed Graphs (TAGs) provide explicit relational structures enriched with textual context, yet often lack semantic depth. Recent research shows that combining LLMs and TAGs yields complementary benefits: enhancing TAG representation learning and improving the reasoning and interpretability of LLMs. This survey provides the first systematic review of LLM--TAG integration from an orchestration perspective. We introduce a novel taxonomy covering two fundamental directions: LLM for TAG, where LLMs enrich graph-based tasks, and TAG for LLM, where structured graphs improve LLM reasoning. We categorize orchestration strategies into sequential, parallel, and multi-module frameworks, and discuss advances in TAG-specific pretraining, prompting, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning. Beyond methodology, we summarize empirical insights, curate available datasets, and highlight diverse applications across recommendation systems, biomedical analysis, and knowledge-intensive question answering. Finally, we outline open challenges and promising research directions, aiming to guide future work at the intersection of language and graph learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Surveys and overviews; Natural language processing; Knowledge representation and reasoning; Graph algorithms"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:28Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yalin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yalin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bishenghui","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Bishenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Confidence-Constrained Cloud-Edge Collaborative Framework for Autism Spectrum Disorder Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:46:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) diagnosis systems in school environments increasingly relies on IoT-enabled cameras, yet pure cloud processing raises privacy and latency concerns while pure edge inference suffers from limited accuracy. We propose Confidence-Constrained Cloud-Edge Knowledge Distillation (C3EKD), a hierarchical framework that performs most inference at the edge and selectively uploads only low-confidence samples to the cloud. The cloud produces temperature-scaled soft labels and distils them back to edge models via a global loss aggregated across participating schools, improving generalization without centralizing raw data. On two public ASD facial-image datasets, the proposed framework achieves a superior accuracy of 87.4\\%, demonstrating its potential for scalable deployment in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21110","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21110","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21110","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elias","familyName":"Bareinboim","name":"Bareinboim, Elias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Confounding Robust Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:58:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key task in Artificial Intelligence is learning effective policies for controlling agents in unknown environments to optimize performance measures. Off-policy learning methods, like Q-learning, allow learners to make optimal decisions based on past experiences. This paper studies off-policy learning from biased data in complex and high-dimensional domains where \\emph{unobserved confounding} cannot be ruled out a priori. Building on the well-celebrated Deep Q-Network (DQN), we propose a novel deep reinforcement learning algorithm robust to confounding biases in observed data. Specifically, our algorithm attempts to find a safe policy for the worst-case environment compatible with the observations. We apply our method to twelve confounded Atari games, and find that it consistently dominates the standard DQN in all games where the observed input to the behavioral and target policies mismatch and unobserved confounders exist."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21110","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:58Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21103","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21103","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21103","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyang","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lailong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lailong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianzhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qianzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangbang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Bangbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deke","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Deke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard T. B.","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Richard T. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensing and Storing Less: A MARL-based Solution for Energy Saving in Edge Internet of Things"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the number of Internet of Things (IoT) devices continuously grows and application scenarios constantly enrich, the volume of sensor data experiences an explosive increase. However, substantial data demands considerable energy during computation and transmission. Redundant deployment or mobile assistance is essential to cover the target area reliably with fault-prone sensors. Consequently, the ``butterfly effect\" may appear during the IoT operation, since unreasonable data overlap could result in many duplicate data. To this end, we propose Senses, a novel online energy saving solution for edge IoT networks, with the insight of sensing and storing less at the network edge by adopting Muti-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL). Senses achieves data de-duplication by dynamically adjusting sensor coverage at the sensor level. For exceptional cases where sensor coverage cannot be altered, Senses conducts data partitioning and eliminates redundant data at the controller level. Furthermore, at the global level, considering the heterogeneity of IoT devices, Senses balances the operational duration among the devices to prolong the overall operational duration of edge IoT networks. We evaluate the performance of Senses through testbed experiments and simulations. The results show that Senses saves 11.37% of energy consumption on control devices and prolongs 20% overall operational duration of the IoT device network."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21103","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21083","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Megahed","name":"Megahed, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atallah","familyName":"Madi","name":"Madi, Atallah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dina El","familyName":"Demellawy","name":"Demellawy, Dina El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian D. C.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Adrian D. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge-Driven Vision-Language Model for Plexus Detection in Hirschsprung's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:42:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hirschsprung's disease is defined as the congenital absence of ganglion cells in some segment(s) of the colon. The muscle cannot make coordinated movements to propel stool in that section, most commonly leading to obstruction. The diagnosis and treatment for this disease require a clear identification of different region(s) of the myenteric plexus, where ganglion cells should be present, on the microscopic view of the tissue slide. While deep learning approaches, such as Convolutional Neural Networks, have performed very well in this task, they are often treated as black boxes, with minimal understanding gained from them, and may not conform to how a physician makes decisions. In this study, we propose a novel framework that integrates expert-derived textual concepts into a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training-based vision-language model to guide plexus classification. Using prompts derived from expert sources (e.g., medical textbooks and papers) generated by large language models and reviewed by our team before being encoded with QuiltNet, our approach aligns clinically relevant semantic cues with visual features. Experimental results show that the proposed model demonstrated superior discriminative capability across different classification metrics as it outperformed CNN-based models, including VGG-19, ResNet-18, and ResNet-50; achieving an accuracy of 83.9%, a precision of 86.6%, and a specificity of 87.6%. These findings highlight the potential of multi-modal learning in histopathology and underscore the value of incorporating expert knowledge for more clinically relevant model outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted into the ICAAI 2025 - The 9th International Conference on Advances in Artificial Intelligence"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21083","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"An","name":"An, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yepeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Bu","name":"Bu, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Reinforcement Learning Framework for Robust and Secure LLM Watermarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T23:53:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:12:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Watermarking has emerged as a promising solution for tracing and authenticating text generated by large language models (LLMs). A common approach to LLM watermarking is to construct a green/red token list and assign higher or lower generation probabilities to the corresponding tokens, respectively. However, most existing watermarking algorithms rely on heuristic green/red token list designs, as directly optimizing the list design with techniques such as reinforcement learning (RL) comes with several challenges. First, desirable watermarking involves multiple criteria, i.e., detectability, text quality, robustness against removal attacks, and security against spoofing attacks. Directly optimizing for these criteria introduces many partially conflicting reward terms, leading to an unstable convergence process. Second, the vast action space of green/red token list choices is susceptible to reward hacking. In this paper, we propose an end-to-end RL framework for robust and secure LLM watermarking. Our approach adopts an anchoring mechanism for reward terms to ensure stable training and introduces additional regularization terms to prevent reward hacking. Experiments on standard benchmarks with two backbone LLMs show that our method achieves a state-of-the-art trade-off across all criteria, with notable improvements in resistance to spoofing attacks without degrading other criteria. Our code is available at https://github.com/UCSB-NLP-Chang/RL-watermark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:38Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21003","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21003","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21003","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Enshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefei","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Xuefei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shengen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guohao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Guohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zinan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T21:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:08:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image Auto-regressive (AR) models have emerged as a powerful paradigm of visual generative models. Despite their promising performance, they suffer from slow generation speed due to the large number of sampling steps required. Although Distilled Decoding 1 (DD1) was recently proposed to enable few-step sampling for image AR models, it still incurs significant performance degradation in the one-step setting, and relies on a pre-defined mapping that limits its flexibility. In this work, we propose a new method, Distilled Decoding 2 (DD2), to further advances the feasibility of one-step sampling for image AR models. Unlike DD1, DD2 does not without rely on a pre-defined mapping. We view the original AR model as a teacher model which provides the ground truth conditional score in the latent embedding space at each token position. Based on this, we propose a novel \\emph{conditional score distillation loss} to train a one-step generator. Specifically, we train a separate network to predict the conditional score of the generated distribution and apply score distillation at every token position conditioned on previous tokens. Experimental results show that DD2 enables one-step sampling for image AR models with an minimal FID increase from 3.40 to 5.43 on ImageNet-256. Compared to the strongest baseline DD1, DD2 reduces the gap between the one-step sampling and original AR model by 67%, with up to 12.3$\\times$ training speed-up simultaneously. DD2 takes a significant step toward the goal of one-step AR generation, opening up new possibilities for fast and high-quality AR modeling. Code is available at https://github.com/imagination-research/Distilled-Decoding-2."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21003","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesimon","familyName":"Barreto","name":"Barreto, Jesimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Caetano","name":"Caetano, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Araujo","name":"Araujo, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Robson","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, William Robson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VESSA: Video-based objEct-centric Self-Supervised Adaptation for Visual Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:07:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have advanced computer vision by enabling strong performance across diverse tasks through large-scale pretraining and supervised fine-tuning. However, they may underperform in domains with distribution shifts and scarce labels, where supervised fine-tuning may be infeasible. While continued self-supervised learning for model adaptation is common for generative language models, this strategy has not proven effective for vision-centric encoder models. To address this challenge, we introduce a novel formulation of self-supervised fine-tuning for vision foundation models, where the model is adapted to a new domain without requiring annotations, leveraging only short multi-view object-centric videos. Our method is referred to as VESSA: Video-based objEct-centric Self-Supervised Adaptation for visual foundation models. VESSA's training technique is based on a self-distillation paradigm, where it is critical to carefully tune prediction heads and deploy parameter-efficient adaptation techniques - otherwise, the model may quickly forget its pretrained knowledge and reach a degraded state. VESSA benefits significantly from multi-view object observations sourced from different frames in an object-centric video, efficiently learning robustness to varied capture conditions, without the need of annotations. Through comprehensive experiments with 3 vision foundation models on 2 datasets, VESSA demonstrates consistent improvements in downstream classification tasks, compared to the base models and previous adaptation methods. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/jesimonbarreto/VESSA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cedegao E.","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cedegao E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cédric","familyName":"Colas","name":"Colas, Cédric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Poesia","name":"Poesia, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua B.","familyName":"Tenenbaum","name":"Tenenbaum, Joshua B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Andreas","name":"Andreas, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code-enabled language models can outperform reasoning models on diverse tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:04:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning models (RMs), language models (LMs) trained with reinforcement learning to produce long-form natural language reasoning, have been remarkably successful, but they still require large amounts of computation and data to train, and can be slow and expensive to run. In this paper, we show that standard instruct LMs can already be elicited to be strong reasoners at a level comparable to or even surpassing their corresponding RMs (e.g., DeepSeek V3 vs R1) without finetuning, across diverse domains from instruction following and creative generation to mathematical reasoning. This is achieved by CodeAdapt, our simple recipe that combines the CodeAct framework, where LMs interleave natural language reasoning with code execution in a multi-step fashion, with few-shot bootstrap in-context learning from as few as five training problems. Analyzing four matched pairs of LMs and RMs, we find that CodeAdapt enables three LMs to outperform the corresponding RMs on average over eight tasks (up to 22.9%) while being 10-81% more token efficient, and delivers superior performance on six tasks when averaged over the four models (up to 35.7%). Furthermore, the code-augmented reasoning traces display rich and varied problem-solving strategies. Our findings support that (1) CodeAdapt-style learning and reasoning may be robust and domain general and (2) code-enabled LMs are cognitively grounded and powerful systems, potentially providing a strong foundation for in-weight reinforcement learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Information Theoretic Learning for Diffusion Models with Warm Start"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:00:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative models that maximize model likelihood have gained traction in many practical settings. Among them, perturbation based approaches underpin many strong likelihood estimation models, yet they often face slow convergence and limited theoretical understanding. In this paper, we derive a tighter likelihood bound for noise driven models to improve both the accuracy and efficiency of maximum likelihood learning. Our key insight extends the classical KL divergence Fisher information relationship to arbitrary noise perturbations, going beyond the Gaussian assumption and enabling structured noise distributions. This formulation allows flexible use of randomized noise distributions that naturally account for sensor artifacts, quantization effects, and data distribution smoothing, while remaining compatible with standard diffusion training. Treating the diffusion process as a Gaussian channel, we further express the mismatched entropy between data and model, showing that the proposed objective upper bounds the negative log-likelihood (NLL). In experiments, our models achieve competitive NLL on CIFAR-10 and SOTA results on ImageNet across multiple resolutions, all without data augmentation, and the framework extends naturally to discrete data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:09Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth Gurunath","familyName":"Shivakumar","name":"Shivakumar, Prashanth Gurunath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Bulyko","name":"Bulyko, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Gandhe","name":"Gandhe, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yile","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Consistent, Effective and Scalable Reasoning Capability in Audio LLMs via Reasoning Process Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T06:18:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The role of reasoning in Audio Large Language Models remains widely underexplored, as introducing a reasoning process often degrades rather than improves performance during inference, a phenomenon we term test-time inverse scaling, where longer reasoning chains yield progressively worse results. We demonstrate that this stems not from fundamental limitations of reasoning itself, but from inadequate training: models without proper guidance for the reasoning process produce hallucinatory, inconsistent reasoning that accumulates errors over longer chains. To address these challenges, we introduce CESAR (Consistent, Effective, and Scalable Audio Reasoners), shifting from outcome verification to rewarding the reasoning process. Our online reinforcement learning framework employs Group Relative Policy Optimization with a multi-faceted reward suite that incentivizes not only correctness and format but also consistency, structured analytical patterns, causal reasoning, domain-knowledge integration, and calibrated reasoning depth. CESAR resolves test-time inverse scaling, transforming reasoning from detriments into gains while revealing model-specific ``reasoning sweet spots\", where performance peaks during test-time scaling. We achieve state-of-the-art results on MMAU Test-mini, substantially outperforming Gemini 2.5 Pro and GPT-4o Audio, and near-human-level performance on MMSU reasoning tasks. Through AI-as-judge evaluations and qualitative comparisons, we provide both quantitative and qualitative validation of our improved reasoning quality. Importantly, enhanced reasoning creates synergistic effects, simultaneously improving multimodal reasoning and perception capabilities. Overall, CESAR establishes a principled method for developing robust and scalable reasoning in Audio LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishaal","familyName":"Udandarao","name":"Udandarao, Vishaal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuankai","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Xuankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Violet Z.","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Violet Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albin Madapally","familyName":"Jose","name":"Jose, Albin Madapally","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fartash","familyName":"Faghri","name":"Faghri, Fartash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Cheng","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Chung-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Centric Lessons To Improve Speech-Language Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spoken Question-Answering (SQA) is a core capability for useful and interactive artificial intelligence systems. Recently, several speech-language models (SpeechLMs) have been released with a specific focus on improving their SQA performance. However, a lack of controlled ablations of pretraining data processing and curation makes it challenging to understand what factors account for performance, despite substantial gains from similar studies in other data modalities. In this work, we address this gap by conducting a data-centric exploration for pretraining SpeechLMs. We focus on three research questions fundamental to speech-language pretraining data: (1) how to process raw web-crawled audio content for speech-text pretraining, (2) how to construct synthetic pretraining datasets to augment web-crawled data and (3) how to interleave (text, audio) segments into training sequences. We apply the insights from our controlled data-centric ablations to pretrain a 3.8B-parameter SpeechLM, called SpeLangy, that outperforms models that are up to 3x larger by 10.2% absolute performance. We hope our findings highlight the impact of effective data curation for speech-language pretraining and guide future data-centric exploration in SpeechLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Tech Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":58,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=created%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22pre-training%22+OR+pretraining+OR+%22post-training%22+OR+%22post+training%22+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22+OR+distillation+OR+quantization%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22pre-training%22+OR+pretraining+OR+%22post-training%22+OR+%22post+training%22+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22+OR+distillation+OR+quantization%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100"}}